        "Antoine Georges",
        "Dieter Vollhardt",
        "Gabriel Kotliar"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Dynamical mean-field theory is a powerful framework for strongly correlated electrons and has reshaped quantitative theory of materials where standard band approaches fail.",
        "attribution": "Georges, Vollhardt, and Kotliar are closely associated with developing and establishing the method; the credit is collective and the work has a broader intellectual history.",
        "maturity": "The framework has been used and extended for decades and has substantial connections to experiments on correlated materials.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "Its Nobel case depends on judging a theoretical method as a discovery of comparable stature to a specific experimental phenomenon."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B022",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Ramamoorthy Ramesh",
        "Sang-Wook Cheong",
        "Yoshinori Tokura"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Magnetically driven ferroelectricity and strong magnetoelectric coupling revealed a compelling route to control electric and magnetic order together in materials.",
        "attribution": "The proposed names represent influential material discovery and experimental work across the field, though the advance emerged through several related compounds and groups.",
        "maturity": "The effects have been reproduced and studied in multiple multiferroic systems, establishing a robust body of physics.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The discovery and credit may be too distributed across materials and teams for a sharply bounded prize."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B049",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Allan H. MacDonald",
        "Pablo Jarillo-Herrero",
        "Rafi Bistritzer"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Magic-angle twisted bilayer graphene exposed tunable correlated and topological quantum matter in a highly controllable two-dimensional platform.",
        "attribution": "Bistritzer and MacDonald supplied the key theoretical prediction, while Jarillo-Herrero's group demonstrated the correlated phases that made the system transformative.",
        "maturity": "The field has generated extensive follow-up results, but its phase diagram and interpretation continue to develop.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "Some reported phases and their microscopic explanations remain contested or sample-dependent."
        ]
      }
    },
